From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Lamprecht <t.lamprecht@proxmox.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 11:46:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] block/rbd: query block status in a worker thread

To answer a block status query for an image with fast-diff, the driver
calls rbd_diff_iterate2(). That call blocks, and before it reports
anything, librbd waits until the requests in flight on the image have
completed. So the query blocks the thread of the image's AioContext.
For a disk without an iothread, that is the main loop, which holds the
BQL meanwhile.

A backup queries the block status of each chunk before copying it,
while up to max-workers reads of other chunks are in flight. Each of
these queries then blocks the main loop until those reads are done.
With slow OSDs, QMP commands can time out, and the vCPUs stall while
they wait for the BQL. In Proxmox VE, this got much more likely once
backups to a Proxmox Backup Server started to process chunks in
parallel, which keeps more reads in flight.

Call rbd_diff_iterate2() in a worker thread of the AioContext's thread
pool, so that the coroutine yields while librbd waits. A query still
waits as long, but the AioContext can handle other requests and events
in the meantime. Reading a backup's data does not get faster, as each
query still waits for the reads in flight before the next read can
start. If the guest writes during a backup, the backup can even take
longer, as the guest no longer stalls and its requests compete with the
backup's.

Test setup: a three-node Ceph cluster with slow OSDs, and a VM whose
8 GiB disk without iothread holds 3 GiB of data. It is backed up with
16 workers to a Proxmox Backup Server in three ways: stopped with data
that the server already has, stopped with new data, and running while
the guest writes. The last row is a stopped VM with a 256 GiB disk that
has 8192 alternating allocated and unallocated runs. Before is
pve-qemu-kvm 11.1.3-3, after is the same with this patch:

                     runs  longest QMP reply    backup time
                           before     after     before    after
  stopped, known      3    436-472ms  1-3ms     4.9-6.0s  5.9-6.9s
  stopped, new data   2    6.2-8.7s   2ms       26-32s    23-31s
  running, writing    3    6.0-10.1s  13-89ms   16s       32-57s
  256 GiB, fragmented 2    246-379ms  3-4ms     33-35s    24-25s

The fragmented image was backed up faster, probably because the main
loop no longer waits for its 65536 queries.

Without this patch, two of the three backups of the running VM failed
with a timeout of 'query-pbs-bitmap-info', so its backup time before is
from one run. Its guest was set to write 4 KiB to a random offset every
20 ms on average, one write at a time, next to other I/O. The 99th
percentile latency of these writes went from 1.2-2.3 s to 0.14-0.44 s,
and the guest completed 15-24 of them per second instead of 3-6.

With an iothread, QMP was not affected before, but the guest's writes
to that disk were, as the iothread blocked instead. With one run each,
the 99th percentile latency of the 4 KiB writes went from 443 to 100 ms,
while the backup took 20 s instead of 12 s.

Signed-off-by: Thomas Lamprecht <t.lamprecht@proxmox.com>
---
 block/rbd.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/block/rbd.c b/block/rbd.c
index 2a70b5a983..d3b6107b18 100644
--- a/block/rbd.c
+++ b/block/rbd.c
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #include "block/block-io.h"
 #include "block/block_int.h"
 #include "block/qdict.h"
+#include "block/thread-pool.h"
 #include "crypto/secret.h"
 #include "qemu/cutils.h"
 #include "system/replay.h"
@@ -120,6 +121,13 @@ typedef struct RBDDiffIterateReq {
     bool exists;
 } RBDDiffIterateReq;
 
+typedef struct RBDDiffIterateCall {
+    rbd_image_t image;
+    uint64_t offs;
+    uint64_t len;
+    RBDDiffIterateReq *req;
+} RBDDiffIterateCall;
+
 static int qemu_rbd_connect(rados_t *cluster, rados_ioctx_t *io_ctx,
                             BlockdevOptionsRbd *opts, bool cache,
                             const char *keypairs, const char *secretid,
@@ -1530,6 +1538,14 @@ static int qemu_rbd_diff_iterate_cb(uint64_t offs, size_t len,
     return 0;
 }
 
+static int qemu_rbd_diff_iterate_worker(void *opaque)
+{
+    RBDDiffIterateCall *call = opaque;
+
+    return rbd_diff_iterate2(call->image, NULL, call->offs, call->len, true,
+                             true, qemu_rbd_diff_iterate_cb, call->req);
+}
+
 static int coroutine_fn qemu_rbd_co_block_status(BlockDriverState *bs,
                                                  unsigned int mode,
                                                  int64_t offset, int64_t bytes,
@@ -1539,6 +1555,7 @@ static int coroutine_fn qemu_rbd_co_block_status(BlockDriverState *bs,
     BDRVRBDState *s = bs->opaque;
     int status, r;
     RBDDiffIterateReq req = { .offs = offset };
+    RBDDiffIterateCall call;
     uint64_t features, flags;
     uint64_t head = 0;
 
@@ -1604,8 +1621,19 @@ static int coroutine_fn qemu_rbd_co_block_status(BlockDriverState *bs,
     bytes += head;
 #endif
 
-    r = rbd_diff_iterate2(s->image, NULL, req.offs, bytes, true, true,
-                          qemu_rbd_diff_iterate_cb, &req);
+    /*
+     * rbd_diff_iterate2() blocks until the requests in flight on the image
+     * have completed, which can take a long time while a backup reads from it.
+     * Call it in a worker thread, so that it does not block the AioContext,
+     * which is the main loop for a disk without an iothread.
+     */
+    call = (RBDDiffIterateCall) {
+        .image = s->image,
+        .offs = req.offs,
+        .len = bytes,
+        .req = &req,
+    };
+    r = thread_pool_submit_co(qemu_rbd_diff_iterate_worker, &call);
     if (r < 0 && r != QEMU_RBD_EXIT_DIFF_ITERATE2) {
         return status;
     }
